.TH RUSTLOGGER 1 "2026-07-23" "rustlogger 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rustlogger \- wrap the current shell in a pseudo\-terminal and log the whole session to a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rustlogger
.RI [ "\-\-log\-dir " dir ]
.br
.B rustlogger
.RI [ "\-\-log\-dir " dir ]
.I command
.RI [ arg " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no arguments,
.B rustlogger
runs your shell attached to a real pseudo\-terminal (pty), exactly the way
.BR script (1)
does, so interactive programs, colors, job control, and
.B sudo
prompts all behave normally. Everything that appears on screen \(em commands
as they're echoed back and all their output \(em is written to a per\-session
log file at the same time, until the session is stopped. It reads the
.B SHELL
environment variable to decide what to run (falling back to
.I /bin/sh
if unset).
.PP
Given a
.I command
(and optionally its arguments), rustlogger instead tracks that one command
headlessly \(em see
.B HEADLESS TRACKING MODE
below.
.PP
Either way, the log by default lands in the directory rustlogger was launched
from; pass
.B \-\-log\-dir
(or set
.BR RUSTLOGGER_LOG_DIR )
to put it somewhere else instead \(em see
.B OPTIONS
below.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-dir\  dir
Write the log file into
.I dir
instead of the current directory,
creating it (and any missing parent directories) if it doesn't already
exist. Must be given before
.IR command ,
if any \(em anything from
.I command
onward belongs to the tracked command's own arguments and is never
reinterpreted, so
.B rustlogger \-\-log\-dir /x mycmd \-\-log\-dir /y
passes the second
.B \-\-log\-dir
through to
.B mycmd
untouched. If both
.B \-\-log\-dir
and
.B RUSTLOGGER_LOG_DIR
are set, this flag wins.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHELL
The shell to run in interactive mode (no
.IR command "),"
falling back to
.I /bin/sh
if unset. Ignored in headless tracking mode.
.TP
.B RUSTLOGGER_LOG_DIR
Default log directory, used when
.B \-\-log\-dir
isn't given. See
.B OPTIONS
above.
.SH STOP CONDITIONS
These apply to the no\-argument, interactive form. Headless tracking mode
(see below) only ever stops via
.B the wrapped shell exits
(there, the tracked command) or
.BR "a caught signal" .
.PP
A session ends, and the log file is closed, when any of the following happens:
.TP
.B the wrapped shell exits
Any exit code (e.g. typing
.B exit
or pressing Ctrl+D).
.B rustlogger
itself then exits with that same code.
.TP
.B the trigger phrase
Typing
.B stoplogger
alone on a line ends the session even if the shell keeps running \(em the
wrapped shell is sent
.B SIGHUP
first, so it isn't left running detached from anything.
.TP
.B a caught signal
.BR SIGINT " (Ctrl+C), " SIGHUP " (the terminal closing), or " SIGTERM
(being killed) delivered to
.B rustlogger
itself all end the session cleanly: the outer terminal's mode is restored,
the wrapped shell is sent
.BR SIGHUP ,
and the log file's footer records which signal it was.
.SH LOG FILE
The log is named
.IR rustlogger\-YYYYMMDD\-HHMMSS.log ,
timestamped (UTC) at session start, and written to the directory
.B rustlogger
was launched from, unless
.B \-\-log\-dir
or
.B RUSTLOGGER_LOG_DIR
says otherwise (see
.BR OPTIONS ).
.B rustlogger
prints the full resolved path (directory included) to stderr when the
session starts. A log looks like:
.PP
.RS
.nf
=== rustlogger session started 2026\-07\-23T14:32:07Z ===
shell: /bin/bash
tty: /dev/pts/4
[2026\-07\-23T14:32:09Z] $ echo hello
[2026\-07\-23T14:32:09Z] hello
[2026\-07\-23T14:32:12Z] $ exit
=== rustlogger session ended 2026\-07\-23T14:32:12Z ===
reason: process exited
exit code: 0
.fi
.RE
.PP
Only what actually appeared on screen is logged, not raw keystrokes. A prompt
that turns terminal echo off for the duration of a sensitive input \(em most
commonly
.B sudo
asking for a password \(em is therefore never captured, since nothing was
ever echoed to log. This is deliberate, not a limitation.
.PP
Everything else on screen
.I is
captured verbatim, so a log should be treated as sensitive: it contains any
secret that was printed, pasted or echoed during the session. Log files are
created mode
.BR 0600 ,
and any directory
.B rustlogger
creates for them mode
.BR 0700 ,
so other local accounts cannot read them.
.SH READING A LOG SAFELY
The transcript is stored byte\-for\-byte, terminal escape sequences included,
so that it is an honest record of what actually crossed the terminal. That
makes a log untrusted content: rendering it re\-executes whatever the logged
programs printed, which can rewrite the window title, clear the screen, write
to the clipboard (OSC 52), use carriage returns so the displayed text differs
from the bytes on disk, or forge lines identical to
.BR rustlogger 's
own
.RB \(lq "=== rustlogger session ended \(em ===" \(rq
footer.
.PP
Prefer
.B cat \-v
or a plain
.BR less ,
both of which render control characters as visible text:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cat \-v rustlogger\-*.log
.fi
.RE
.PP
Avoid a bare
.B cat
or
.BR "less \-R" ,
which pass the escape sequences through to your terminal. This matters most
when reviewing a transcript of something you did not run yourself \(em a CI
job, a build script, or anything started by another person or an agent.
.SH HEADLESS TRACKING MODE
.B rustlogger
.I command
.RI [ arg " ...]"
runs
.I command
(with its arguments) attached to a pty, the same as interactive mode, but
touches no outer terminal at all: no raw terminal mode, and no
.B stoplogger
phrase detection, since there's no live keystroke stream to watch for it.
Output is logged exactly as in interactive mode and also mirrored to
.BR rustlogger 's
own stdout. The session ends when
.I command
exits on its own, or when
.B rustlogger
itself is sent
.BR SIGINT ", " SIGHUP ", or " SIGTERM
(the tracked command is sent
.B SIGHUP
first, so it isn't left running).
.PP
This mode exists for driving
.B rustlogger
from something other than a live terminal \(em see the
.B rustlogger\-mcp\-server
project alongside this one, which lets Claude start a tracked command in the
background, check its log later, and stop it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B the wrapped shell's (or tracked command's) own exit code
When the session ends because it exited on its own.
.TP
.B 0
When the session ends via the
.B stoplogger
trigger phrase.
.TP
.B 128+n
When
.B rustlogger
itself is stopped by signal
.IR n ,
following the usual shell convention (e.g. 130 for
.BR SIGINT ).
.TP
.B 1
On an unexpected I/O error, or if the outer terminal's input closes out from
under the session.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Start a logged interactive session with your normal shell:
.B rustlogger
.TP
Start a logged interactive session with a specific shell:
.B SHELL=/bin/bash rustlogger
.TP
Track a build in the background and log it, headlessly:
.B rustlogger npm run build
.TP
Track a build, keeping logs out of the repo's own working directory:
.B rustlogger \-\-log\-dir ~/.rustlogger/logs npm run build
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
Linux only.
.IP \(bu 2
Not a replacement for full asciinema\-style timing/playback \(em this is a
plain\-text transcript, not a recorded\-and\-replayable format.
.IP \(bu 2
The log's filename itself isn't configurable, only its directory \(em always
named after the UTC start time.
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B stoplogger
trigger is matched against everything typed, with no way to tell which
program is actually consuming it. Typing or pasting
.B stoplogger
alone on a line into an editor, a pager, a heredoc or an interactive database
client ends the session too.
.IP \(bu 2
No terminal window\-resize
.RB ( SIGWINCH )
propagation to the wrapped shell yet.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR script (1),
.BR bash (1),
.BR sh (1)
